1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/114656477/peter-paulson-teig
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Paul Olson Teig and Britta (Bertha) Knutsdatter Flogstad
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Cora Lura. The children to this union are listed on Cora's record.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT Obituary transcribed by Dena Gray-Fisher, source: Roland Record, June 20, 1968.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter P. Teig, 86, died at Marshalltown
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter P. Teig died June 11, 1968, at the Iowa Soldiers Home at Marshalltown, where he had been a patient for the past six years. He had been in failing health for several years, having had both legs amputated. He was born April 10
2 CONC , 1882, the son of Paul and Britta Teig, and was 86 years, 2 months of age at the time of his death. He was baptized and confirmed in Bergen Lutheran Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Teig had lived all his life in the Roland community. He operated a harness and shoe repair shop for many years, and served several years in the U.S. Army during World War I, seeing service in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was united in marriage to Cora Jeannette Lura on November 8, 1920 in Roland. He was a member of the Salem Lutheran Church, and had been active in the American Legion, of which he was a past commander, and in the Roland Fire Dep
2 CONC artment, having served as chief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Teig was preceded in death by his parents; four sisters, Mrs. Carrie Haugen, Gusta Teig, Gurina Teig and Julia Teig; and two brothers, Ole and Knut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include his wife, Cora; two sons, Carrol of Rosemount, Minn., and Earl of Bloomington, Minn.; two daughters, Pearl (Mrs. Charles B. West) of Oskaloosa, and Nadine (Mrs. Ervin E. Smith) of Scottsdale, Ariz.; one brother, 
2 CONC Benhard Teig of Des Moines; and one sister, Amanda (Mrs. C. J. Henryson) of Rochester, Minn.; and eight grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral service was held in Salem Lutheran Church at Roland on Friday, June 14, with Pastor Everett Nelson officiating. Orris Osheim sang "Softly and Tenderly", and Harris Twedt jr. sang "How Great Thou Art".
2 CONT
2 CONT Interment was in the Roland Cemetery. Military rites were conducted by the Roland American Legion. Pallbearers were Palmer Teig, Mons Teig, Hubert Teig, Boyd Teig, Victor McKinney and Eugene Lura, nephews of the deceased.
