1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Egenes 
2 CONT Photo added by Nbowers
2 CONT
2 CONT John Egenes
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT 4 Mar 1867
2 CONT Norway, Benton County, Iowa, USA
2 CONT Death
2 CONT 26 Sep 1942 (aged 75)
2 CONT Des Moines, Polk County, Iowa, USA
2 CONT Burial
2 CONT Story City Cemetery
2 CONT Story City, Story County, Iowa, USA
2 CONT Plot
2 CONT North Central Section, Row 26, Plot 10
2 CONT Memorial ID
2 CONT 102717546 · View Source
2 CONT Memorial
2 CONT Photos 1
2 CONT Flowers 1
2 CONT
2 CONT From Story City Herald October 1, 1942 (page 1 & 8)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of John Egenes
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral of Well Known Old Time Citizen Was Held in Roland Monday Afternoon
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Egenes, for many years a resident of Story City, succumbed to the sickness from which he had suffered for several months, his death occurring at the Lutheran hospital in Des Moines last Saturday, following an operation.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He reached the age of 75 years and 6 months.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held in Roland Monday, conducted by Rev. Allen E. Nelson at the home and in the Bergen Lutheran church, in which he had been confirmed by Rev. Jacobson in the fall of 1881. The casket bearers were: Sven J. Ne
2 CONC ssa of Story City, Osmund Thompson and Wm. Cunningham of Roland, Carroll Richardson, Maurice Nissen and Fritz Krause, all of Renwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Relatives and Friends Present
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Nellie and Ellen Anderson of Minneapolis; Mr. and Mrs. Melvin Anderson of McIntyre; Mr. and Mrs. Ove Anderson of Thor; J. T. Anderson of Des Moines; Mr. and Mrs. Otis Anderson, Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Anderson of Blue Earth, Minn.; Se
2 CONC vert Anderson of Mankato, Minn.; Oscar Anderson and Mr. and Mrs. George Reng of Sioux Rapids; Mr. and Mrs. Adolph Krause, Mrs. Bird Thiel, Mrs. Rose Hefty, Mr. and Mrs. M. Yanney, Mrs. R. McGowan, Dr. and Mrs. W. H. Long, Mrs. M. 
2 CONC B. Richardson, Mrs. A. Trumell, Mrs. Frances Krause, Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Nissen, Mrs. Don McGowan and Mrs. Gerhard Meyer, all of Renwick; John Hensel, Mr. and Mrs. F. Breckenfelder, Mr. and Mrs. F. C. Mohr, Mr. and Mrs. J. Minglin, R
2 CONC ev. and Mrs. E. . Fritz, all of Auburn; Rev. J. H. Reentz of Parkersburg; Rev. and Mrs. L. W. Reuther of Sheffield; Mr. and Mrs. Sam Guddall and Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Sandvig of Thor; Ruby Haskell, Mrs. Lester Chambers, Mrs. C. O. Me
2 CONC yers all of Des Moines.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT All the children of the family were able to be present, besides ten grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Egenes Obituary
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Egenes was born at Norway, Benton county, Iowa, March 4, 1867, a son of Andrew and Margaretha Egenes. The family home continued continued in Benton county until 1881, when they moved to Story county, settling on a farm near M
2 CONC cCallsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There the young man attended local school and later high school in Nevada, and finally Iowa State Teachers college at Cedar Falls. He taught school for a time, but in the meantime had learned the telegraphic trade and in 1891 beca
2 CONC me depot agent for what was then the Iowa Central, at the station in McCallsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He was united in marriage to Emma Louise Anderson in November, 1891, and soon after was transferred to Story City where he served as agent for the Iowa Central for 19 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Mrs. Egenes died in 1907 leaving the husband with seven children between the ages of 10 days and 14 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It was on November 26, 1911, that he was united in marriage to Martha Anderson of Roland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Quitting the railroad work for a time, he engaged in the hardware business at Story City, in which he continued until about five years ago, when he retired and moved back to Story county, locating in Roland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Failing in health recently he was finally taken to the Iowa Lutheran hospital, Des Moines, where he received care and treatment, but passed away Saturday, Sept. 26.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his wife, Mrs. Martha Egenes, and seven children, as follows: Iver A. of Story City; Mrs. Geo. Thompson of Ostrander, Minn.; Mrs. W. J. Hensel of Auburn; Julius of Sutherland; Byron of Los Angeles; Harold of Dele
2 CONC van, Wis., and Mrs. Lowell Neveln of Des Moines.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He also leaves 18 grandchildren, 2 great grandchildren and one sister, Mrs. Tom Johnson of McCallsburg.
