2 CONT https://nye.digitalarkivet.no/source/2232/parish/b70147a1-648d-4fe8-af33-6cc3899ba109
2 CONT
2 CONT '''FT1920''':
2 CONT https://media.digitalarkivet.no/view/73962/4136
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Bio notes: Reiste først til Montana, deretter videre til Juneau, Alaska. Her jobbet han i gullgruvene sammen med brødre og fettere. Årsak til emigrasjon var "liden fortjeneste". I 1906, gården til familien hans var evidentisk i då
2 CONC rlig stand og søsteren hans Jorunn var blind.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han jobbet rundt fire år i USA som gruvearbeider fra 1906-1910 da han kom tilbake til gården. Da hadde faren hans Vrål død.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hjemmet ble påbygd etter i 1911 og i 1947 ble det laget et fjøs av murstein der.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amund var gift med Valborg Gurine Undeland, datter av Ola Undeland (f. Årtun) og Anna.
2 CONT Amund og Valborg fikk totalt 8 barn.
2 CONT
2 CONT De hadde 3 kalver, 3 kuer, hest, høns, 2 griser og 15 sauer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det var masse folk som bodde her i 1920 og i boken (Norges bebyggelse : Sørlige seksjon Herredsbindet for Rogaland Nordre del) på side 742 (skrevet som 722 i boka) kan du se tekst for Øverland Gnr 41, der de bodde hvor all denne i
2 CONC nformasjonen ligger.
2 CONT https://www.nb.no/items/e035834c39d97b691152721102059001?page=741
2 CONT
2 CONT Info om Amunds reise til Amerika:
2 CONT på "Massachusetts, U.S., Arriving Passenger and Crew Lists, 1820-1963" står det info om ankomsten hans i USA:
2 CONT
2 CONT Name	Amund V Overland
2 CONT Gender	Male
2 CONT Nationality	Norwegian
2 CONT Marital Status	Single
2 CONT Arrival Age	26
2 CONT Birth Date	abt 1880
2 CONT Departure Place	Liverpool, England
2 CONT Last Residence Place	Sande
2 CONT Final Destination Place	Helena, Massachusetts
2 CONT Residence Place	Norway
2 CONT Arrival Date	11 May 1906
2 CONT Arrival Place	Boston, Massachusetts, USA
2 CONT Money	$25
2 CONT Ship	Ivernia
2 CONT Friend	Johannes Overland
2 CONT Person in Old Country Residence Place	Helena Montana
2 CONT Person in US Name	Johanna Aarsland
2 CONT Person in US Relationship	Cousin
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.theradiohistorian.org/Kiny/KINY-20d.htm 
2 CONT "A view of Juneau looking south from downtown in the 1930's. The gutted Goldstein Building in the foreground is the former location of the KINY studios. The building was destroyed by fire in 1939 and later rebuilt. In the distance
2 CONC , the Alaska Juneau Mine can be seen rising up the mountains on the left, and a peninsula of rock tailings from the mine can be seen extending out into the Gastineau Channel. KINY built their second antenna and transmitter site ne
2 CONC ar the end of the peninsula. The dumping of rock on this peninsula was discontinued in the 1930's, and it was later dumped by barge into the middle of the channel. Operations at the A-J mine were discontinued in 1944."
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juneau,_Alaska
2 CONT "Mining era and naming
2 CONT After the California gold rush, miners migrated up the Pacific Coast and explored the West, seeking other gold deposits. In 1880, Sitka mining engineer George Pilz offered a reward to any local native in Alaska who could lead him 
2 CONC to gold-bearing ore. A local native arrived with some ore, and several prospectors were sent to investigate. On their first trip to Gold Creek, they found deposits of little interest. However, Pilz sent Joe Juneau (the cousin of M
2 CONC ilwaukee co-founder Solomon Juneau) and Richard Harris back to the Gastineau Channel, directing them to go to Snow Slide Gulch (the head of Gold Creek). According to the Rev. Samuel Young, in his book Alaska Days with John Muir, J
2 CONC uneau and Harris decided to explore their party's campsite at the creek head in the summer of 1879. They found nuggets "as large as peas and beans" there, in Harris' words.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 18, 1880, the two men marked a 160-acre (650,000 m2) town site and soon a mining camp sprang up. Many miners arrived within a year and the camp became a village, albeit made up mostly of tents and shacks rather than bui
2 CONC ldings. It was the first European American settlement founded in the territory after the United States purchased Alaska. By the autumn of 1881, the village had a population of over 100 and was known as Rockwell, after Lt. Com. Cha
2 CONC rles Rockwell; later it was known as Harrisburg after prospector Richard Harris. On December 14, 1881, it was decided at a miners' meeting of 72 persons to name the settlement Juneau, after prospector Joe Juneau.[15][16]"
2 CONT
2 CONT "Development of mining
2 CONT Prospector and placer miner John Lemon operated at the time in what is today the Lemon Creek area. The neighborhood which developed there was given his name by early settlers, several other landmarks in Juneau have also been named
2 CONC  for him. Major mining operations in the Juneau mining district prior to World War II included the Treadwell Mine, the Alaska-Juneau Mine, and the Alaska-Gastineau Mine.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1906, after the decline of whaling and the fur trade, Sitka which was the original capital of Alaska, had become less important and the territorial legislature moved the seat of government to Juneau in accordance with a 1900 fe
2 CONC deral law.[8] Juneau was the most populous city in Alaska during the inter-war years, surpassing Fairbanks population by the 1920 census. Anchorage became the largest city in terms of population in 1950."
